# Reload-surviving watches

## Decision

Active `herdr_watch` actors and degraded `herdr_ping_wait` child processes survive Pi `/reload` without keeping old runtime resources alive.

On `session_shutdown` with `reason: "reload"`, Bellwether appends one versioned suspension entry to the current Pi branch. The entry carries the complete serializable watch input and its absolute expiry. Bellwether then cancels the old XState actors, closes their exact sockets, aborts fallback children, and drains completion.

On `session_start` with `reason: "reload"`, the replacement extension instance reads only the newest suspension entry and starts fresh actors with the same IDs. It computes the remaining timeout from the original absolute expiry, so repeated reloads cannot extend a deadline. New, resumed, and forked sessions restore nothing.

An empty newest entry prevents an older suspension from winning. A malformed newest entry also restores nothing. Failing closed is safer than reviving stale wake policy.

## State boundary

Reload is suspension, not a new watch state. Each runtime actor still follows:

```text
starting -> running -> matched | timedOut | targetGone | failed | cancelled
```

The old actor ends as `cancelled` while shutdown suppresses its wake. The replacement actor begins from the persisted input. Durable suspension owns continuity; XState owns one runtime generation.

## Regression boundary

- `src/suspension.test.ts` checks versioned round trips and newest-entry authority.
- `src/pi-bellwether.test.ts` runs direct and degraded waits across simulated reloads, verifies stable IDs, and checks that the resumed direct timeout is smaller than the original deadline.

## Explicit resume after a process restart (2026-09-21)

`quit` now writes the same suspension entry as `/reload`. It restores nothing on its own: a fresh process that resumes the session file starts with no waits, and `/herdr-resume` reads the newest entry only when the operator asks. `withoutExpired` in `src/suspension.ts` drops waits whose absolute deadline passed while no process owned them; `restoreSuspended` in the extension skips ids that are already active. Session replacement (`new`, `resume`, `fork`) still writes nothing.

Why: two sessions that had been open for a week carried 528k and 156k pre-fix presence entries (`bellwether-intercom-signal` with kind `capability` or `binding`, stopped by 994c545). Each process held a 1 GB heap and sat at 80 to 99 percent CPU on GC while idle. Cleaning them needed a restart, and a restart lost every watch, so the watches had to be re-armed by hand from the suspension record. `src/chatter.ts` now counts that chatter on every non-reload start and `scripts/strip-intercom-chatter.mjs` removes it with the id/parentId tree re-linked and verified.

Regression boundary additions: `src/chatter.test.ts`, the `withoutExpired` case in `src/suspension.test.ts`, and three cases in `src/pi-bellwether.test.ts` (quit writes and `/herdr-resume` restores; session replacement writes nothing; startup warns about chatter once and stays quiet on a clean branch).
